##########################################################################################################

Here’s how to fix it, in the order that works most often.

1) Confirm whether the remote actually received anything

Run:

git status
git log --oneline --decorate -5
git ls-remote --heads origin


If your latest commit hash is not on origin/main, the push didn’t make it.

2) Find what’s making the push 536MB (biggest offenders)

This lists the biggest blobs in your repo history:

git rev-list --objects --all |
  git cat-file --batch-check='%(objecttype) %(objectname) %(objectsize) %(rest)' |
  awk '$1=="blob"{print $3"\t"$4}' |
  sort -n |
  tail -20


If you see .terraform/ provider exe or big PDFs/videos, that’s why.

3) If ANY .terraform or binaries are in history, remove them properly (best fix)

You already hit this earlier: .gitignore won’t fix history.

Install filter-repo (if needed)
pip install git-filter-repo

Strip terraform caches from history
git filter-repo --path-glob "**/.terraform/**" --invert-paths


Optional (also strip provider binaries everywhere):

git filter-repo --path-glob "**/terraform-provider-*" --invert-paths
git filter-repo --path-glob "**/*.exe" --invert-paths


Then force push:

git push origin main --force


This usually turns a 536MB push into a tiny one.

4) If you really are clean but the push is still large: make Git more tolerant

These help with flaky HTTPS pushes:

git config --global http.postBuffer 524288000
git config --global http.lowSpeedLimit 0
git config --global http.lowSpeedTime 999999


Then try again:

git push


(These don’t fix GitHub size limits — they only reduce disconnect pain.)

5) Switch to SSH (often fixes curl/SSL issues on Windows)
Check remotes
git remote -v

Change origin to SSH
git remote set-url origin git@github.com:ChuckKeyes/Obsidian.git


Then:

git push


If you haven’t set up SSH keys yet, GitHub has a quick setup flow, but try the history-cleaning first because your pack size screams “big files”.

6) Make sure your .gitignore is actually excluding what you think (going forward)

From repo root, run:

git check-ignore -v AWS/Class-Notes/Week-14/Week14-Sunday/.terraform/providers/registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws/5.100.0/windows_amd64/terraform-provider-aws_v5.100.0_x5.exe


If it prints a matching rule, ignore is fine.
If it prints nothing, your pattern isn’t matching that path.

